# Elaboration: the surface language into core terms (adamas-elab).
#
# A literal brace is written in Fluent as {"{"} and {"}"}.

mono-runaway = more than { $limit } specialisations: generic code calls itself at ever new types, and monomorphisation does not terminate
mono-refused = the specialisation does not typecheck: { $error }
warn-unused-implicit = name `{ $name }` from an implicit group is never used: in this position `{"{"} { $name } : … {"}"}` reads as a binder, and a record is written `{"{"} { $name } : … | r {"}"}` (§4.2)
warn-defaulted-literal = using default { $name }; add an annotation for an explicit type (§4.3)
warn-unused-binding = `{ $name }` - { $what } - is never read; if that is intended, name it `_{ $name }`
warn-discarded-result = a result of type `{ $ty }` is discarded; if that is intended, write `let _ = …`
warn-unused-import = `{ $name }` is imported from `{ $module }`, but never used
elab-unknown-name = name `{ $name }` not found
elab-foreign-abi = ABI { $abi } is not supported: level 1 FFI knows only "C" (§5.3)
elab-foreign-label = `extern` marks the call with the `Foreign` effect (§5.3), and the label is not declared: write `effect Foreign`
elab-foreign-type = { $why } (§5.3)
elab-export-target = `export` over `{ $name }`: { $why } (§5.3)
elab-export-type = { $why } (§5.3)
elab-export-blocked = { $said }
elab-export-evidence = effect `{ $label }` in an export position: its operations are resolved by the evidence vector, and a level 1 callback carries no environment - a function pointer has none. The evidence vector travels to the foreign side in `userdata`, and that is level 2 (§5.3)
elab-not-a-constructor = `{ $name }` is not a constructor: in a pattern a capitalised name matches, a lowercase one binds
elab-sealed-constructor = `{ $name }` is not visible: `{ $data }` is declared in a sealed module, and its representation is hidden
elab-hidden-member = `{ $name }` is hidden by sealing: the module signature does not name it
elab-uppercase-binding = `{ $name }` does not bind: a capitalised name refers to a declaration, a lowercase one binds
elab-graded-sort = a multiplicity parameter has no multiplicity of its own
elab-repeated-grade = `{ $name }` is declared as a multiplicity parameter twice
elab-field-grade = a record member's multiplicity position takes a parameter name, not an expression over them
elab-mixed-grade = a product and a sum do not mix in one multiplicity position: write `ω` - the definition will pass, but the binding stops being polymorphic
elab-repeated-summand = a summand is repeated in a sum of multiplicities: `q + q` is not `q`, and there is no way to write it
elab-misplaced-product = a product of multiplicities is written before the bound name and has no multiplicity of its own
elab-unknown-factor = `{ $name }` is not a multiplicity parameter: a product is written only with them
elab-repeated-binding = `{ $name }` appears twice in a clause: equality of arguments is not expressed by a pattern
elab-owned-carrier = `{ $owned }` cannot be substituted for parameter `{ $parameter }` of `{ $callee }`: a value of this type is used there { $carrier } times, and ownership requires exactly one
elab-owned-holder = `{ $family }` is not a `resource` and cannot hold `{ $owned }`: nobody would close it
elab-no-implicit-parameter = there is no inferred parameter here: `@` writes what would otherwise be inferred
elab-empty-case = `case` without branches: nothing to match
elab-not-updatable = only a record can be updated
elab-attribute = attribute `@{ $name }` is not checked: { $why }
elab-not-total = `{ $name }` is declared `@total`, but the verdict is negative: an argument decreases neither by matching nor by value, or something non-total is called
elab-class-head = a class head is a name with arguments: `class Eqv a`, `instance Eqv Nat`
elab-no-instance = instance `{ $written }` not found
elab-rigid-instance = an instance of `{ $class }` is not chosen for a type variable: its dictionary comes only from the context - add `{"{"}{ $class } …{"}"} =>` to the signature; for a default method, declare `{ $class }` a superclass
elab-partial-instance = instance `{ $written }` is not total and cannot appear in a type or a proof
elab-instance-depth = resolving `{ $written }` failed: the nesting limit of instance contexts ({ $limit }) is exhausted, and the chain of goals does not decrease
elab-declaring-instance = instance `{ $written }` is being declared right now, and its dictionary is built only on its own parameters: there is nothing to discharge a goal of another shape
elab-projecting-head = the head of the `{ $class }` argument is a synonym returning its parameter: it has no key, write the type it names
elab-trailing-default = parameter `{ $name }` has no default, but the previous one has: only trailing parameters have defaults
elab-not-an-effect = `{ $name }` is not an effect label: a label must end in the sort `Effect`
elab-row-parameter = a family's row parameter - `{ $name }` of sort `Effect` - comes in a later slice; today a `data` parameter is a type
elab-sealed-constraint = signature `{ $signature }` cannot seal: `{ $member }` carries `{ $class } { $param }`, and `{ $param }` is a parameter of the sealed `{ $sealed }`; move the constraint into a module parameter or declare `{ $class }` `coherent`
elab-member-multiplicity = member `{ $method }` is checked at multiplicities { $passing }, but the field promises { $promised }: an instance must keep the field's promise in full
elab-sealed-instance = the instance context carries `{ $class } { $param }`, and `{ $param }` is a parameter of the sealed `{ $sealed }`; move the constraint into a module parameter or declare `{ $class }` `coherent`
elab-coherent-duplicate = instance `{ $written }` is already declared, and `{ $class }` { $why }
elab-undetermined-head = an instance of `{ $class }` cannot be chosen: the argument type is not determined in time for the search - give it an annotation (§4.3)
elab-missing-type-member = the instance does not set type member `{ $name }`: it has no default, write `type { $name } = …`
elab-coherent-orphan = instance `{ $written }` is declared outside the file of class `{ $class }` and outside the files of its argument heads, and `{ $class }` { $why }
elab-coherent-context = the context of instance `{ $class }` carries `{ $context }`, which is not declared `coherent`
elab-abstract-type = `type { $name }` without an equation declares a member of a module signature; there is nowhere to take the type from here
elab-module-member = `{ $name }` is not declared in { $what }: { $why }
elab-unknown-module = module `{ $path }` not found: looked for `{ $file }`
elab-undeclared-dependency = module `{ $path }` is covered neither by the importing file's package nor by its dependencies: a package imports only what its manifest declares
elab-private-module = module `{ $path }` of package `{ $package }` is not exported: from outside only what its manifest lists in `exports` can be imported
elab-import-cycle = import cycle: { $through }
elab-in-module = module `{ $path }` does not check
elab-not-exported = module `{ $module }` does not declare `{ $name }`
elab-prelude-clash = name `{ $name }` comes both from the prelude and from `{ $module }`: declare your own, do not open the other one, or opt out of the prelude
elab-not-matchable = the type of the matched value is not inferred: give it
elab-block-without-value = the block ends with a binding: it has no value
elab-unrestricted-owned = a binding of { $owned } type cannot be ω: values of such a type do not exist at ω
elab-owned-top-level = `{ $name }` is declared with an owned type ({ $owned }): only a binding of multiplicity 1 can hold it
elab-owned-discarded = the statement's value has an owned type ({ $owned }) and is discarded: bind it to a name
elab-written-row-tail = tail `{ $name }`: { $why }
elab-local-definition = local definition `{ $name }`: { $why }
elab-reserved-operation = `{ $name }` is the name of a handler's value branch, an operation cannot be named that
elab-fixity = operator `{ $operator }`: { $why }
elab-handler-branch = branch `{ $name }`: { $why }
elab-handler-label = label `{ $name }`: { $why }
elab-nothing-to-mask = `mask` has nothing to mask: the surrounding row names no labels
elab-not-handled = under handler `{ $effect }`: { $why }
elab-handler-state = `state` of handler `{ $effect }`: { $why }
elab-owned-state = `state` of type `{ $name }`: the thread is bound at ω, and an owned type has no values at ω (§3.3)
elab-multi-over-holder = `{ $name }` holds a resource, and the `handleMulti` resumption is multi-shot: the destructor would run twice (§3.4)
elab-multi-over-nursery = `{ $name }` starts a nursery, and the `handleMulti` resumption is multi-shot: resuming again would not replay its fibers (§3.4, §5.2)
elab-multi-with-unknown = the computation under `handleMulti` is built in place: there is nobody to ask whether it holds a resource or a nursery (§3.4)
elab-multi-with-resource = `{ $name }` is a resource in the scope of `handleMulti`: a multi-shot resumption would run its destructor twice (§3.4)
elab-resource-without-drop = resource type `{ $name }` has no destructor: a definition in the body is required (§3.3)
elab-destructor-shape = destructor `{ $name }` must take one `{ $data }` of multiplicity 1 and return a type that does not depend on it and is not owned itself
elab-scope-bound = `{ $name }` is bound to its scope and { $face }: a closure that captured ownership can be applied or passed as an argument, but not carried out
elab-destructor-without-body = `{ $name }` in the body of `{ $data }` is declared without a body: a bare signature is a constructor, and constructors are capitalised
elab-shared-destructor = `{ $name }` is already the destructor of `{ $first }`: the namespace is flat, give the destructor of `{ $data }` its own name
elab-resource-member = only `drop` is defined in the body of `{ $data }`, not `{ $name }`
elab-missing-signature = `{ $name }` has clauses, but no signature
elab-detached-signature = the signature of `{ $name }` is separated from its clauses: another declaration stands between them
elab-missing = { $what }
elab-core = { $error }
elab-clauses = { $error }
elab-not-fbip = `{ $name }` is declared `@fbip`, but it is not compatible: { $fault }
elab-flat-shape = { $why }
elab-primitive-shape = { $why }
elab-not-flat = `Flat` is not derived for { $ty }: { $why }
elab-allocates = `{ $name }` is declared `@noalloc`, but it { $blame }
elab-sealed-operation = `{ $name }` is not visible: `{ $effect }` is declared in a sealed module, and only that module handles it
elab-reserved-name = name `{ $name }` is taken by the language: { $what } (§4.11), and you cannot declare your own under it
elab-literal-type = the literal does not fit type `{ $ty }`: { $why }
elab-literal-range = the literal does not fit in `{ $ty }`
elab-untyped-literal = literal `{ $text }` has no type: neither a number nor a primitive is expected here - write the type with a binding (`let n : Int32 = { $text }`)
elab-unary-literal = a number greater than { $limit }: without a primitive type a literal unfolds in unary (§4.3), and its term is as deep as the number - write `Int64` or another primitive
elab-string-length = the string does not fit: it has { $length } bytes, and the limit is { $limit } - beyond that the machine does not keep an array as a block (§4.11); a longer buffer is built with `arrayNew` and `arraySet`
elab-string-zero = a zero byte inside a string: byte #{ $seen } is zero, and the compiler appends the terminating zero itself; the foreign side reads up to the first zero (§5.3) and will see { $seen } of `Array { $length } UInt8`, not { $content } - bytes with an inner zero are written with `arrayNew` and `arraySet`
elab-ambiguous-instance = there are several instances `{ $written }` ({ $candidates }); choose one with `using`
elab-owned-field = `{ $constructor }` takes a field of type `{ $field }` ({ $owned }), so `{ $data }` must be declared `{ $keyword } { $data }`
decl-1 = the program is assembled from a single text, there is nowhere to look
decl-2 = the language has no such attribute
decl-3 = a functor body is instantiated at every application, and instance uniqueness does not hold there (§3.5)
decl-4 = class methods are top-level names, and a module qualifies them
decl-5 = a module
decl-6 = an imported file
decl-7 = a module
decl-8 = group members are declared by a single call, and it cannot qualify them
decl-9 = a linker symbol is one per program, and a module member's name is qualified
decl-10 = a functor
decl-11 = a default is filled in by the written arity, and a functor member also carries the functor's parameters
decl-12 = class `Flat` is declared with one parameter and the single method `layout : Layout` (§4.11)
decl-13 = class `Primitive` is declared with one parameter and the single method `simdLayout : Layout` (§4.9)
decl-14 = a `Flat` instance is not written by hand: the compiler derives it structurally from the type's representation (§4.11)
decl-15 = a `Primitive` instance is not written by hand: the class is closed, and its instances are exactly the ten primitives of §4.11 (§4.9)
decl-16 = the program
decl-17 = an anonymous instance for this type is already declared; several for one type are written as named instances
decl-18 = a class
decl-19 = `@total`, `@noalloc` and `@fbip` on a method would promise a verdict for every instance, but the verdict is computed per definition - write the attribute on the instance member
decl-20 = a class
decl-21 = a class carries method signatures and their defaults
decl-22 = is declared `coherent`
decl-23 = carries a type member (§4.1)
decl-24 = an instance
decl-25 = the method's type is written in the class, so an instance carries method clauses and type member values (`type T = …`)
decl-26 = an instance
decl-27 = the class has no such type member
decl-28 = an instance
decl-29 = the class has no such method
decl-30 = a class
decl-31 = the method's type depends on the value of another method, and an instance has nothing to infer it from
decl-32 = a `mutual` group
decl-33 = a constructor type may not name a definition of the block - families are declared before definitions, and the signature does not have them yet; move it into a separate declaration before the block, ordered scoping allows that. A family of the group can be named
decl-34 = a `mutual` group
decl-35 = a member's type may not name a sibling - the types of all members are checked before the group is declared; move it into a separate declaration before the block, ordered scoping allows that. A family of the group can be named
decl-36 = a `mutual` group
decl-37 = a group carries definitions with signatures and families; modules and classes are declared separately
decl-38 = a `mutual` group
decl-39 = a group without members declares nothing
decl-40 = a module signature
decl-41 = a parameter makes a function of an interface, and a signature is the interface itself
decl-42 = a module signature
decl-43 = an ascription checks a module against an interface, and a signature is the interface itself
decl-44 = a functor body
decl-45 = the parameter has the same name as the enclosing one, and substitution looks parameters up by name, so the outer one would become unreachable
decl-46 = a module with an expression body
decl-47 = a parameter is declared on a module with a block of members
decl-48 = a module with an expression body
decl-49 = a signature with an effect member requires a block of members: an effect is not declared in an expression body
decl-50 = it is a primitive type
decl-51 = it is the built-in array
decl-52 = it is a region area (§3.6)
decl-53 = it is a handle inside an area (§3.6)
decl-54 = it is a foreign pointer (§5.3)
decl-55 = it is a primitive operation
decl-56 = a module
decl-57 = the signature declares an effect, and an effect of the same name must be a member of the module
decl-58 = a module
decl-59 = the label's telescope differs from the one declared in the signature
decl-60 = a module signature
decl-61 = operations are an effect's representation, and a signature declares a label without them
decl-62 = a module signature
decl-63 = the label is written above its declaration (ordered scoping, §4.8)
decl-64 = a module signature
decl-65 = an equation on a type member makes the signature translucent, and there are none yet (§10 question 46)
decl-66 = a module signature
decl-67 = a signature carries declarations without implementations
decl-68 = `...` stands in the result position: the variadic part is the tail of the argument list, and it cannot be returned
decl-69 = `...` is not in the arrow chain of the declaration: in C the variadic part is the tail of the top-level argument list, and it has no boundaries, neither two nor one inside parentheses
decl-70 = `...` comes first: a variadic C function declares at least one named parameter - the one `va_start` names
decl-71 = a multiplicity expression is not written in a binder of a variadic declaration: how many names `(q x : a)` binds is decided by the declaration of `q`, and positions before `...` must be counted as written
decl-72 = an implicit does not cross the C boundary: it has no runtime value, and level 1 cannot carry a polymorphic argument
decl-73 = an erased parameter does not cross the C boundary: it has no runtime value (§3.3), yet its type crosses the boundary - the foreign side would get one argument fewer than written
decl-74 = a callback crosses the C boundary as a pointer to an exported definition: its parameters and result must be machine words, and it has no nested callback, buffer, unit or erased binder
decl-75 = the parameter does not cross the C boundary: level 1 carries a machine word - a primitive of §4.11, `CPtr`, a flat `Array n T` or unit; a struct by value needs knowledge of the platform ABI
decl-76 = the result does not cross the C boundary: level 1 takes a machine word or unit (`void`)
decl-77 = the position of `...` in the declaration does not agree with the elaborated type: the count of explicit parameters differs, and the boundary of the variadic part cannot be guessed - the call ABI depends on it
decl-78 = an argument after `...` is declared with a type whose width C changes: a narrow integer travels as `int`, `Float32` as `Float64`, and that is part of the ABI, not a liberty of the call. Write the promoted type: the language has no width conversions at all (§4.3)
decl-79 = the linker symbol is the written name, and a C identifier consists of letters, digits and underscores and does not begin with a digit
decl-80 = there is no definition of that name above (§4.8: declare before calling)
decl-81 = a definition is exported, not a constructor, an operation or a label
decl-82 = it has no body: a postulate or a foreign symbol has nothing to hand out
decl-83 = the symbol is already taken by another export: there are no two `define`s of one name
decl-84 = an implicit is not exported: the foreign side does not write it, and nobody can infer it on a call from C
decl-85 = an erased parameter is not exported: it has no runtime value (§3.3), and the foreign side will write an argument in this position
decl-86 = an export parameter must be a machine word - a primitive of §4.11 or `CPtr`: a buffer cannot be handed out (the foreign side gives a bare address, and no Perceus header can be built around it), and a unit has nothing to produce it
decl-87 = an effect on an intermediate arrow: the foreign side calls the export in full, and it never makes a partial application
decl-88 = a function is exported, not a value: a value has no call site where the foreign side would get the result
decl-89 = an export's result must be a machine word: a unit cannot be released - its value is a Perceus object, and a `void` function will not drop it
decl-90 = an effect on an intermediate arrow: a foreign function performs `Foreign` in full when it is applied in full
decl-91 = only a flat array crosses the C boundary (§4.11): the cells of a pointer array are Perceus objects with headers, and the foreign side understands none of them
decl-92 = group
decl-93 = fixity
decl-94 = import
decl-95 = a functor
decl-96 = a functor member is lifted under its parameters, and a label does not carry them
